SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEKEND READ
Saturday, March 21, 2026

Visit enough mid-size factories and you meet him: the veteran who knows that the bin count on line 4 is always fifty short, that the Tuesday supplier ships on Wednesday, that the machine the system says is available is down every third shift for a reason nobody documented. The name changes; the role does not. Every scheduling and inventory system in the building is, in practice, a suggestion that this person corrects.

The software industry has spent thirty years trying to replace him and has mostly failed, for two reasons. The first is data: his knowledge is exactly the set of facts that never got into the ERP, because entering them was nobody's job. The second is trust: when the system and the veteran disagree, the plant manager sides with the veteran, because the veteran has been right before and the system has not.

The newest wave of plant-floor tools is taking a different approach. Instead of replacing his judgment, they try to capture it: they watch what he overrides, ask why, and feed the answer back. The pitch to the plant manager is not "the system is smarter than Dave"; it is "the system remembers what Dave knows when Dave retires," which is a pitch plant managers, staring at an average workforce age of 51, actually respond to.

Whether this works at scale is the open question of the decade for industrial software. What is clear from the deployments we have seen is that the tools that ship a line-side tablet and a way to say "no, because" get used, and the ones that ship a dashboard for the VP do not.

